{
  "description": "`and`, `or`, `mod` and `is` are legal dictionary keys, so a worded operator opening a line inside a dict literal opens the NEXT ENTRY rather than continuing the previous entry's value (issue #933). Both separator spellings are covered — spaced (`mod: 2`, `and : 3`, `or:4`) and glued (`mod:two`, which the lexer emits as one `variable` token). The glued form is the one that matters: both readings parse cleanly, so misreading it would change the dictionary silently rather than raise a diagnostic. Every entry is read back through a place expression because a dict's printed form does not show its entries. The last case is the opposite direction and is what makes this fixture fail on a parser without the fix: with no separator after it the same word IS an operator, so `{ a: 5` / `mod 3 }` is ONE entry whose value is `5 mod 3` = 2.",
